@*
    The dashboard: productivity and cost, narrowable to one repository.

    This used to be an empty pane in the Monitoring module, where a dashboard was a
    derived view over a signal stream nobody had written yet. It is its own context
    now, and the wording has moved with it — nothing here derives from Monitoring's
    Progress Signal; every figure comes from a provider that already holds it.

    Two things this pane deliberately is not:

    It is not configurable. There is no layout to edit, nothing to add or remove,
    and no preference that outlives the surface being closed. The composition below
    is the composition, which is why a stale dashboard cannot happen here — there is
    no saved arrangement to fall out of step with the code.

    It is not a coordinator. It awaits nothing and holds no figures. Every part
    below starts its own fetch, carries its own status, and refreshes on its own, so
    an unconfigured Anthropic key delays and explains one part rather than blanking
    six. All this pane owns is the filter, and passing it down.

    The filter drives the productivity parts and visibly does not drive the cost
    parts, because neither provider reports spend per repository. That is stated on
    each cost part rather than left for a reader to work out from a figure that
    refuses to move.

    The ✕ only reports that it was pressed; what that closes is the shell's business.
*@

<section @key=@("dashboard-pane") class="dashboard-panel" id="dashboard-pane" aria-labelledby="dashboard-title" data-testid="dashboard-panel">
    <header class="dashboard-panel__header">
        <div>
            <p class="dashboard-panel__eyebrow">Dashboard</p>
            <h2 id="dashboard-title" class="dashboard-panel__title">Productivity and cost</h2>
            <p class="dashboard-panel__subtitle">Your own pull requests, issues and rework, and what your assistants cost.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="dashboard-panel__header-actions">
            <IconButton AriaLabel="Close dashboard" CssClass="btn--ghost" OnClick="() => OnClose.InvokeAsync()">✕</IconButton>
        </div>
    </header>

    <div class="dashboard-panel__filters" data-testid="dashboard-filters">
        <div class="dashboard-filter">
            <span class="dashboard-filter__label" id="dashboard-repository-label">Repository</span>
            @* A select rather than a segmented strip. The strip is what the
               storybook prototyped over five fixture repositories; a real
               configuration is however many somebody has added, and a one-of-many
               control that can overflow the header is the wrong shape for a list
               whose length is not this pane's decision. *@
            <RepositorySelector CurrentValue="@_scope.RepositoryAlias"
                                Options="@RepositoryOptions"
                                Label="Repository to focus on"
                                IncludeEmptyOption="true"
                                EmptyLabel="All repositories"
                                TestId="dashboard-repository-filter"
                                OnChanged="FocusRepository" />
        </div>

        <div class="dashboard-filter">
            <span class="dashboard-filter__label">Window</span>
            <ButtonGroup AriaLabel="How far back to look" TestId="dashboard-window-filter">
                @foreach (var period in Periods)
                {
                    @* PressedChanged rather than the pressed state itself: the strip
                       is one-of-many, so this pane owns the selection and the button
                       only announces the press. *@
                    <ToggleButton Pressed="_scope.Period == period"
                                  PressedChanged="_ => ChooseWindow(period)"
                                  TestId="@($"dashboard-window-{Weeks(period)}")">
                        @($"{Weeks(period)} weeks")
                    </ToggleButton>
                }
            </ButtonGroup>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="dashboard-panel__content" id="dashboard-content">
        <div class="dashboard-section" data-testid="dashboard-productivity">
            <h3 class="dashboard-section__title">Productivity</h3>
            <HeadlinePart Scope="_scope" />
            <div class="dashboard-section__split">
                <ScorePart Scope="_scope" />
                <ReworkPart Scope="_scope" />
            </div>
            <TrendPart Scope="_scope" />
        </div>

        <div class="dashboard-section" data-testid="dashboard-cost">
            <h3 class="dashboard-section__title">Cost</h3>
            <p class="dashboard-section__note">
                Your own assistant usage. Neither Anthropic nor GitHub reports spend per repository,
                so the repository filter above does not change anything in this section.
            </p>
            <SpendThisMonthPart Scope="_scope" />
            <div class="dashboard-section__split">
                <SpendTrendPart Scope="_scope" />
                <SpendByModelPart Scope="_scope" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    /// <summary>The two windows on offer. A closed set, because an arbitrary one
    /// would make two readings taken a fortnight apart incomparable.</summary>
    private static readonly DashboardPeriod[] Periods =
    [
        DashboardPeriod.FourWeeks,
        DashboardPeriod.TwelveWeeks
    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// What the dashboard is looking at. A field rather than anything persisted:
    /// closing the surface forgets it, which is the whole reason there is nothing to
    /// configure here.
    /// </summary>
    private DashboardScope _scope = DashboardScope.Default;

    [Inject]
    private IRepositoryDirectory Repositories { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Raised when somebody dismisses the dashboard. Whether that returns
    /// to the workspace, opens something else or does nothing is the shell's
    /// decision — the pane only reports that the ✕ was pressed.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnClose { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Read on every render rather than cached, so a repository added in Settings
    /// while the dashboard is open turns up in the filter.
    /// <para>
    /// The label is the <c>owner/name</c> form rather than the alias. Two
    /// repositories can carry aliases that look alike — <c>backlog</c> and
    /// <c>backlog-ide</c> — and the full name is what makes the option
    /// unambiguous; the alias is still the value, because that is what the scope
    /// and the entry syntax use.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<SelectorOption> RepositoryOptions =>
        [.. Repositories.Repositories.Select(repository =>
            new SelectorOption(repository.Alias, repository.FullName))];

    /// <summary>
    /// Focuses one repository, or all of them when the empty option is chosen.
    /// <para>
    /// The scope is replaced rather than mutated, because every part decides whether
    /// to re-fetch by comparing the scope it last fetched for against the one it was
    /// given. A record with value equality makes that comparison correct; mutating a
    /// shared instance would make every part think nothing had changed.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private void FocusRepository(string? alias) =>
        _scope = _scope with { RepositoryAlias = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(alias) ? null : alias };

    private void ChooseWindow(DashboardPeriod period) => _scope = _scope with { Period = period };

    private static int Weeks(DashboardPeriod period) => new DashboardScope(Period: period).Weeks;
}
